MODULE documentation, last updated on 11 Sep 2026

A module is a shared object the server loads at run time.  It can add
commands, user modes and channel modes, and attach to points in the life
of a user or a channel, in some cases deciding whether the server carries
on.


READ THIS FIRST
===============

A module runs INSIDE the server process and shares its address space.

  * A module that crashes takes the whole server with it.
  * A module that corrupts memory corrupts the server's memory.
  * A module can read anything the server can, including the TLS private
    key and every client's IP address.
  * A module that blocks stalls every connected client, because the server
    is single-threaded.

There is no sandbox and no way to add one without changing what a module
is.  Load only modules you have read and trust, from people you trust.
This is an operational decision, not a technical one, and the server
cannot make it for you.


WRITING A MODULE
================

A module exports exactly one symbol, a struct ModuleInfo named
ircu_module.  Everything else the server needs, it reaches through that.

  #include "config.h"
  #include "module.h"

  static int example_init(struct ModuleHandle *mod)
  {
    /* register commands, modes and hooks here */
    return 0;                 /* non-zero refuses to load */
  }

  static void example_fini(struct ModuleHandle *mod)
  {
    /* release your own resources; the server reverts its own */
  }

  struct ModuleInfo ircu_module = {
    IRCU_MODULE_ABI,
    "example",                /* name, as /MODULE UNLOAD takes it */
    "1.0.0",                  /* version */
    "you <you@example.org>",  /* author */
    "One line about what it does",
    example_init,
    example_fini,             /* may be NULL */
    NULL                      /* mi_rehash, may be NULL */
  };

mi_init runs once after the module loads.  Returning non-zero aborts the
load, and the server unloads the module again -- use it when the module
cannot do its job, for instance because a command name it needs is taken.

mi_fini runs once before the module unloads.  Anything registered through
the module API is reverted by the server whether or not mi_fini does it,
so most modules can leave it NULL.

mi_rehash runs on /REHASH when the module stays loaded.  If the module's
file changed on disk, the server unloads and reloads it instead, and
mi_rehash is not called.


THE ABI
=======

IRCU_MODULE_ABI is compared exactly.  A module built against a different
value is refused with an entry in the log.

There is no backwards compatibility, on purpose.  When the ABI changes,
modules are recompiled.  A mismatched pointer layout in a shared address
space is not a class of failure worth being lenient about, and the
alternative -- a module quietly reading the wrong field -- is far worse
than one that will not load.

Check the ABI a module was built against with /STATS M.


BUILDING
========

Modules live under modules/, organised by kind, and are built by the same
CMake run as the server:

  modules/<type>/<name>.c      a module self-contained in one file
  modules/<type>/<name>/       a module built from a directory

<type> is a directory name that only organises the tree -- commands,
modes, hooks and workers are the ones in use, and a new one is just a new
directory.  The type is not part of a module's name: the module is named,
loaded and unloaded by <name> alone, so a name may appear under one type
only, and the build refuses to configure if it appears under two.

Dropping mymodule.c into modules/<type>/ is all it takes for a module in
one file: modules/CMakeLists.txt calls ircu_add_modules(), which finds it
and builds it as the module "mymodule".  The globs are re-run by the
build, so a new file is picked up by `cmake --build build` without
configuring again.

A module that outgrows one file becomes a directory, modules/<type>/
mymodule/, and is still named after it.  Everything below the directory
is sorted by suffix:

  *.c              compiled into mymodule.so, subdirectories included
  *.h              the module's own headers; the directory is on the
                   include path, and nothing outside the module sees them
  anything else    a resource: copied next to the shared object at build
                   time and installed with it, at the same relative path

A resource is anything the module wants to read at run time -- a word
list, a template, a default configuration.  The module finds it through
module_dir(), which is the absolute directory the shared object was loaded
from:

  snprintf(path, sizeof(path), "%s/words.txt", module_dir(mod));

Read it in mi_init, not from a hook: a module loads at startup or on an
operator's request, when reading a file is fine, and never in the middle
of the message flow, where it is not.

Either way the module links nothing: the server's symbols are resolved
against the ircd executable at load time, which works because the ircd is
built with ENABLE_EXPORTS.  The build tree mirrors the source tree, with a
shared object where the sources were:

  build/modules/<type>/mymodule.so
  build/modules/<type>/mymodule/mymodule.so     + the resources

and `cmake --install` puts the same shape under the module directory --
the IRCU_MPATH build setting, which defaults to a "modules" subdirectory
of the data directory (DPATH), so it follows DPATH into a chroot.  That is
the directory the server loads from, so an installed module needs no
further configuration to be found.

To check what a module needs from the core:

  nm -u mymodule.so | grep -v GLIBC


LOADING
=======

A module is always named, never pathed.  The name is the file name of the
shared object without its directory and without the ".so" suffix; the
server looks for it in every type directory of the module directory it
was built with (MOD_PATH in config.h, from IRCU_MPATH), as either
<type>/<name>.so or <type>/<name>/<name>.so.  A name containing a "/" is
refused, so a configuration file cannot reach a shared object outside that
directory; a shared object dropped directly into the module directory,
outside any type, is not found either.  A name that matches under two
types is refused rather than resolved to whichever came first.

From the configuration file:

  Module {
   name = "mymodule";
  };

("file" is accepted as a spelling of "name"; it takes the same bare module
name.)

On /REHASH a module still listed stays loaded; one no longer listed is
unloaded; one whose file changed on disk is unloaded and loaded again.

Or at run time, with the module_admin privilege:

  /MODULE LIST
  /MODULE LOAD mymodule
  /MODULE UNLOAD mymodule
  /MODULE RELOAD mymodule

The nick of the operator that loads a module is recorded and shown by both
listings.  It is a copy taken at load time, so it survives that client
disconnecting, and it does not follow a later nick change.

All four take the module's name, the same one the configuration file uses.
That is the file name, which need not be the name the module declares in
its ModuleInfo; /MODULE LIST prints the file name first, then the declared
name in parentheses, then where the module was found as a path relative to
the module directory -- "modules/hooks/nocaps.so" -- and never the absolute
path, which says where the server keeps its files on the host and is not
something to hand out over IRC.  /STATS M shows the same relative path.

Everything except LIST also requires the CONFIG_OPERCMDS feature, since it
changes what code the server is running.

If a RELOAD unloads the module but cannot load it again, the server says
so plainly: the module is then not loaded at all.


COMMANDS
========

  MessageHandler handlers[LAST_HANDLER_TYPE];

  handlers[UNREGISTERED_HANDLER] = NULL;      /* NULL becomes m_ignore */
  handlers[CLIENT_HANDLER]       = m_mycmd;
  handlers[SERVER_HANDLER]       = NULL;
  handlers[OPER_HANDLER]         = m_mycmd;
  handlers[SERVICE_HANDLER]      = NULL;

  module_add_command(mod, "MYCMD", "MYCMD", MAXPARA, MFLG_SLOW, handlers);

The handler signature is the same one every command in ircd/m_*.c uses:

  int m_mycmd(struct Client *cptr, struct Client *sptr,
              int parc, char *parv[]);

Note what the parameters argument means.  It is the MAXIMUM number of
parameters to split the line into, not a minimum: anything past that many
arrives in the last one, as though it had been introduced with a colon.
Pass MAXPARA unless your command takes free-form trailing text.  Check
your own minimum in the handler with need_more_params().

A module cannot replace a command the core already has.  Registering a
name or token that is taken fails, and returning non-zero from mi_init is
the usual response.


USER MODES
==========

A module can add a user mode -- a letter users set on themselves with
/MODE, that the server carries in the client's flags and propagates across
the network like any core mode.

  static flag_t my_flag;

  if (!module_add_user_mode(mod, 'P', &my_flag))
    return -1;                /* the letter is taken, or none are left */

The module does not choose the bit.  Two modules that both picked, say,
bit 3 would silently be the same mode, and neither author would ever see
it happen -- so the server hands the bits out, and a bit freed by an
unload is handed out again.  Keep what you are given; it is not stable
across a reload.

Read and write it on a client with the same macros the core uses:

  if (HasUFlag(cptr, my_flag))    /* is the mode set? */
  SetUFlag(cptr, my_flag);        /* set it   */
  ClrUFlag(cptr, my_flag);        /* clear it */

Registering the letter is half of a mode.  The server will accept /MODE
+P, show it in /MODE and in RPL_MYINFO, and send it to the other servers,
but it means nothing until a hook reads the flag and acts on it -- see
modules/modes/umode_nopm.c, which registers +P and then refuses private messages
to a user who has it.

A mode letter is A-Z or a-z, nothing else.  Registration fails if the core
already owns the letter, if another module took it first, or if the module
asks twice.  There is nothing useful to do about that but refuse the load:
a module cannot do its job under a letter its users were not told about.

The mode goes away with the module.  On unload the server unregisters the
letter and takes the mode off every user that still has it, announcing an
ordinary "-P" to them and to the network, so nobody is left believing a
mode nothing implements any more is still in force.  A module can also
give a mode back while it stays loaded:

  module_del_user_mode(mod, 'P');

which does the same stripping.  A module can only remove its own modes --
not the core's, and not another module's.

Two things to keep in mind:

  * A module mode is global.  It is propagated to the rest of the network,
    where a server without the module loaded will not recognise the letter
    and will answer the sender with ERR_UMODEUNKNOWNFLAG.  A mode is
    something every server on the network runs, or something no server
    runs; keeping the module list the same everywhere is the network
    operator's job.

  * The core's rules about who may set a mode apply to core modes only.
    A module mode can be set and cleared by any local user, exactly as
    asked.  A module that wants a say in that takes
    HOOK_CLIENT_PRE_UMODE, which sees the whole requested mode string
    before any of it is applied, and can refuse it.

Modes are counted and listed by /STATS M alongside commands.


CHANNEL MODES
=============

A module can add a channel mode too -- a letter chanops set on the channel
with /MODE, that the server carries in the channel's modes and propagates
across the network like any core mode.

  static chanmode_t my_flag;

  if (!module_add_chan_mode(mod, 'G', &my_flag))
    return -1;               /* the letter is taken, or is not a letter */

Unlike a user mode, the bit is not handed out: it follows from the letter.
'A' to 'Z' are bits 0 to 25 and 'a' to 'z' are bits 26 to 51, which is the
same rule the core's own modes follow.  So the mode a module registers is
the same bit on every server that loads the module, and two servers built
from the same sources agree on it without exchanging a word.  Keep what
you are given anyway: reading the bit from the letter yourself is writing
the rule down twice.

Read and write it on a channel with the same macros the core uses:

  if (HasCFlag(chptr, my_flag))   /* is the mode set? */
  SetCFlag(chptr, my_flag);       /* set it   */
  ClrCFlag(chptr, my_flag);       /* clear it */

The registered modes are readable as a list:

  const struct ChanMode *cm;

  for (cm = channel_chan_modes(); cm; cm = cm->next)
    ...                           /* cm->c, cm->flag, cm->attr */

It is a pointer to const, and it is the server's list: a module reads it,
and changes it only through module_add_chan_mode() and
module_del_chan_mode().

Registering the letter is half of a mode.  The server will accept /MODE
#channel +G from a chanop, show it in /MODE and in RPL_MYINFO, advertise
it in the CHANMODES token of RPL_ISUPPORT and send it to the other
servers, but it means nothing until a hook reads the flag and acts on it
-- see modules/modes/cmode_nocaps.c, which registers +G and then refuses shouted
messages to a channel that has it.

A mode letter is A-Z or a-z, nothing else.  Registration fails if the core
already owns the letter, if another module took it first, or if the module
asks twice.

The mode goes away with the module.  On unload the server unregisters the
letter and takes the mode off every channel that still has it, announcing
an ordinary "-G" to the members and to the rest of the network, so that no
channel is left believing it enforces a policy nothing implements any
more.  A module can also give a mode back while it stays loaded:

  module_del_chan_mode(mod, 'G');

which does the same stripping.  A module can only remove its own modes.

Two things to keep in mind:

  * A module's channel mode is global, and unloading the module on one
    server clears the mode everywhere.  A channel belongs to the network,
    not to one server: leaving the letter set on servers that still have
    the module, while this one no longer honours it, would be a channel
    that half-enforces a rule.  Two servers are expected to run the same
    sources, and by the same convention to load the same modules.

  * A module's mode is a plain flag on the channel: it takes no argument,
    it is set and cleared by chanops like +m or +n, and the core does not
    ask anything else about it.  A module that wants a say in who may set
    it takes HOOK_CHANNEL_PRE_MODE, which sees the whole requested mode
    string before any of it is applied, and can refuse it.

Channel modes are counted and listed by /STATS M alongside commands and
user modes.


CLIENT CAPABILITIES
===================

A module can add an IRCv3 capability -- a name a client negotiates with
CAP LS and CAP REQ, and that the server then knows is in force for that
connection.

  static int my_cap;

  if (!module_add_cap(mod, "example.org/react", 0, &my_cap))
    return -1;               /* name taken, malformed, or no slot left */

Unlike a channel mode, the position is handed out and does NOT follow from
the name: the server gives you the lowest free one and it can differ
between servers, between restarts and between loads.  Keep what you are
given.  A capability is negotiated with one client over one connection and
never crosses a server link, so there is nothing for two servers to agree
on and nothing is imposed.

Read it on a client with the macros the core uses:

  if (CapActive(cptr, my_cap))    /* did this client negotiate it? */
  if (HasCap(cptr, my_cap))       /* did it ask for it? */

The registered capabilities are readable as a list, sorted by name:

  const struct Capability *cap;

  for (cap = cap_first(); cap; cap = cap->cap_next)
    ...                  /* cap->cap_name, cap->cap_index, cap->cap_flags */

It is a pointer to const, and it is the server's list: a module reads it,
and changes it only through module_add_cap() and module_del_cap().

A name is what IRCv3 allows: letters, digits, '-', '.' and '_', optionally
prefixed by a vendor and a single '/'.  Use a vendor prefix of your own --
"example.org/react" -- or the "draft/" prefix for a specification that has
one.  Registration fails if the core already has the name, if another
module took it first, if the module asks twice, or if every position is
taken (there are CAP_MAX of them, and the core's come first).

Registering the name is half of a capability.  The server will advertise
it in CAP LS, accept CAP REQ for it and remember that the client has it,
but it means nothing until the module reads the flag and behaves
differently -- sends an extra tag, an extra numeric, a message the client
would not otherwise get.

Two things the server does for you, and you should not do by hand:

  * Registering on a running server sends CAP NEW to every client that
    negotiated cap-notify, so a capability that appears mid-session is
    announced rather than merely becoming true.

  * The capability goes away with the module.  On unload the server sends
    CAP DEL and clears the capability from every local client that had it,
    so no client is left believing a capability is in force that nothing
    implements any more -- which would have it go on sending what the
    server can no longer understand.  A module can give one back while it
    stays loaded:

      module_del_cap(mod, "example.org/react");

    which does the same.  A module can only remove its own.

A module that wants to take its capability out of service without
unregistering it -- a backend it depends on went away, say -- uses:

  cap_update_availability(my_cap, 0);   /* CAP DEL; stops being advertised */
  cap_update_availability(my_cap, 1);   /* CAP NEW; advertised again */

and one that advertises a value to CAP LS 302 clients ("name=value") sets
it with cap_set_value(my_cap, "..."); the core does this for
draft/languages.

Capabilities are counted by /STATS M alongside commands, user modes and
channel modes.


HOOKS
=====

  module_add_hook(mod, HOOK_CLIENT_PRE_NICK, my_hook,
                  HOOK_PRIORITY_DEFAULT, NULL);

The callback:

  static enum HookResult my_hook(struct HookContext *ctx, void *user);

Return HOOK_CONTINUE to abstain, HOOK_ALLOW to permit and stop the chain,
or HOOK_DENY to refuse.  HOOK_PENDING says "hold this, I will answer
later"; see SUSPENDING A HOOK below.

Hooks of the same type run in priority order, lowest first, with ties
broken by load order.  The chain stops at the first hook that does not
return HOOK_CONTINUE.

A HOOK_DENY cannot be overturned by a hook further down the chain.  If it
could, whether a veto held would depend on which module happened to load
first.

A hook may unregister itself from inside its own call.

The hook points, and what each one gets:

  Hook                    hc_client      hc_source   hc_channel  hc_arg
  ----------------------  -------------  ----------  ----------  ------------
  CLIENT_PRE_REGISTER *   the client     its link    -           nick
  CLIENT_REGISTERED       the client     its link    -           -
  CLIENT_PRE_NICK *       the client     its link    -           new nick
  CLIENT_NICK_CHANGED     the client     its link    -           old nick
  CLIENT_PRE_UMODE *      the client     its link    -           mode string
  CLIENT_EXITING          the client     the killer  -           quit reason
  CHANNEL_PRE_CREATE *    the client     its link    -           channel name
  CHANNEL_PRE_JOIN *      the client     its link    the channel channel name
                                                     (NULL if new)
  CHANNEL_JOINED          the client     -           the channel channel name
  CHANNEL_PARTED          the client     -           the channel channel name
  MESSAGE_PRE_CHANNEL *+  the sender     the sender  the channel message text
  MESSAGE_PRE_PRIVATE *+  the recipient  the sender  -           message text
  MESSAGE_RECEIVED        the service    the sender  the channel message text
                          bot                        (NULL if
                                                     private)
  SERVER_LINKED           the server     -           -           server name
  CONFIG_LOADED           -              -           -           -

  * can veto        + can rewrite

MESSAGE_RECEIVED fires for a PRIVMSG or NOTICE sent to a service bot of
this server -- a client with user mode +S that this server introduced
(see include/bot.h and doc/readme.services) -- and, for every service
bot on a channel, for each message to that channel.  The sender may be
on any server.  hc_notice says which of the two it was.  The message is
not delivered anywhere else: a bot has no connection to deliver to, so
the hook is how its module hears it.

CONFIG_LOADED fires once at start-up, after the configuration has been
read and the server is ready to introduce clients, and again at the end
of every rehash.  It is the point for a module that keeps state derived
from the configuration: mi_init and mi_rehash both run in the middle of
the parse, when the blocks after the module's own Module{} block have
not been read yet.

Hooks marked as vetoing run before the server acts, so refusing leaves
nothing to undo.  The rest are notifications: their return value is
ignored.

Some hook points listed in enum HookType are declared but not yet wired
into the core -- CHANNEL_PRE_PART, CHANNEL_PRE_MODE, CHANNEL_PRE_TOPIC,
CHANNEL_DESTROYED and SERVER_SPLIT.  Registering for one of them succeeds
and simply never fires.


SUSPENDING A HOOK
=================

A module that has to ask something slow -- a database, a password hash on
a worker thread, a process of its own -- cannot answer from inside the
hook.  The main thread is the one thread that touches core state, so a
blocking call there stalls every other client on the server.  Such a hook
suspends the operation instead:

  static enum HookResult on_register(struct HookContext *ctx, void *user)
  {
    if (!ctx->hc_token)              /* cannot wait here; decide now */
      return HOOK_CONTINUE;

    ask_somebody_else(ctx->hc_token, cli_name(ctx->hc_client));
    return HOOK_PENDING;
  }

and answers when it knows:

  module_hook_resume(mod, token, HOOK_ALLOW, NULL);
  module_hook_resume(mod, token, HOOK_DENY, "that nickname is reserved");

Four things to know:

  * hc_token is non-zero only where the server has a way back in.  Today
    that is HOOK_CLIENT_PRE_REGISTER, which is held beside ident, DNS, CAP
    negotiation and the PING cookie -- registration was already something
    the server waits for.  A HOOK_PENDING anywhere else is logged and read
    as HOOK_CONTINUE, so a module that does not check hc_token is a module
    whose policy silently does not apply.

  * Suspending stops the chain, the way HOOK_ALLOW and HOOK_DENY do.  The
    module that suspended owns the decision.

  * The hold has a deadline -- FEAT_HOOK_TIMEOUT, ten seconds by default
    -- and expiring counts as HOOK_DENY.  A module that holds a veto point
    is deciding whether something may happen; letting it through because
    the module never answered would be the outcome it was asked to
    prevent.  Unloading the module while it owes an answer ends the same
    way.

  * Resume from the main thread, once per token.  A worker thread must not
    call it: the result comes back through the task's wt_done, which the
    main thread runs, and that is where the answer belongs.  Resuming a
    token the server no longer holds -- the client quit, the deadline
    passed -- returns zero and does nothing, which is a normal outcome and
    not an error to guard against.

The context is the core's stack and is gone by the time the answer
arrives, so a module copies what it needs out of it before returning
HOOK_PENDING, and the reason for a refusal travels as an argument of
module_hook_resume() rather than in hc_reason.

modules/hooks/slowauth.c is the reference: it holds a registration, asks
a worker thread, and resumes.


SASL MECHANISMS
===============

The core speaks the AUTHENTICATE protocol and brings PLAIN and EXTERNAL.
A mechanism whose exchange the core cannot know in advance -- SCRAM, a
one-time-password step, OAUTHBEARER against an identity provider -- is a
module:

  #include "sasl.h"

  static enum SaslStep my_step(struct SaslSession *ses, const char *in,
                               size_t inlen);

  module_add_sasl_mechanism(mod, "SCRAM-SHA-256", 0, my_step);

A step reads the decoded bytes the client sent and either fills in
SaslSession::ss_out with a challenge and returns SASL_STEP_CHALLENGE, or
fills in ss_authcid, ss_authzid and ss_secret and returns
SASL_STEP_CREDENTIAL.  SASL_STEP_FAIL ends the exchange.

Four things to know:

  * A mechanism decides the *shape* of the exchange, never the answer.
    Whether the credential is good is the identity provider's business,
    and that is a separate registration; see include/account.h.  A
    mechanism that checked a password itself would be one that works on
    the server it was written for and nowhere else.

  * The name is uppercased and validated (RFC 4422: 1 to 20 characters of
    A-Z, 0-9, '-' and '_'), so "scram-sha-256" and "SCRAM-SHA-256" are one
    mechanism and cannot both be registered.

  * Pass SASL_MECH_NEEDS_TLS if the exchange puts a secret on the wire in
    the clear, as PLAIN does.  The server then refuses it on a plaintext
    connection before the client sends anything.

  * The session holds a password.  Do not copy it anywhere that outlives
    the exchange, and do not log it.  The server wipes the session with
    ircd_crypto_wipe() on every way out, including the client hanging up
    halfway through.

Registrations are reverted on unload, like everything else, and a module
cannot remove the core's mechanisms or another module's.


THE IDENTITY PROVIDER
====================

One module answers two questions for the whole server: is this credential
good, and whose nickname is this?

  #include "account.h"

  static void my_verify(account_id_t id, const struct AccountRequest *req);
  static void my_lookup(account_id_t id, const char *nick);
  static void my_cancel(account_id_t id);

  static const struct AccountProvider provider = {
    "identity", my_verify, my_lookup, my_cancel
  };

  module_add_account_provider(mod, &provider);

Neither call may block.  They start the work -- a query through db.h, the
password hash on a worker (see WORKER THREADS and ircd_pwhash.h) -- and
return; the answer goes back later, in the main thread, with
account_complete() or account_complete_owner(), exactly once per question
accepted.  Answering from inside the call is allowed, for a provider with
a cache.

Five things to know:

  * One provider at a time.  A second registration is refused rather than
    replacing the first, so two identity modules in ircd.conf are an error
    and not a coin flip.

  * A question the core has dropped -- the client left, the deadline
    passed -- is not an error to answer: account_complete() says the
    handle is no longer outstanding and does nothing.

  * The request does not outlive the call, and its secret is a password.
    Copy what is needed and nothing more; the core wipes the secret as
    soon as the call returns.

  * "Could not be established" is not "free".  A lookup that fails --
    database unreachable, no answer in time -- comes back as
    ACCOUNT_NICK_UNKNOWN, and a caller must not read that as a nickname
    nobody has registered.  The one moment an impostor gets somebody
    else's nickname is the moment the service protecting it is not
    answering, and that moment can be arranged.

  * Unloading the module fails every question in flight before the code
    is unmapped, so nothing waits for an answer that is never coming.


THE CACHE
=========

Any module may use the cache; one module implements it.  See
doc/readme.cache for the whole of it, and include/cache.h for the API.

  module_cache_get(mod, "nick:maria", on_result, data);
  module_cache_set(mod, "nick:maria", json, len, 300, NULL, NULL);
  module_cache_del(mod, "nick:maria", NULL, NULL);

The handle is passed so that the calls are dropped if the module unloads
before they are answered.

A zero return means there is no cache -- no driver, or no Redis{} block --
and it needs no branch of its own: the cache is never the truth, so that
is the same thing as a miss.  Read the cache first, read the database
second, cache the misses as well as the hits, and delete the key after
writing to the database rather than waiting for the expiry.

To implement one instead, register a struct CacheDriver with
module_add_cache_driver().  One driver at a time, it must never block the
main thread, and it answers with cache_complete() exactly once per call.


COMMAND HOOKS
=============

HOOK_COMMAND_PRE and HOOK_COMMAND_POST are the two points every command
passes through.  They are what lets a module see one user act on another
-- KICK, KILL, WHOIS, INVITE, MODE, SILENCE, GLINE, SLINE, JUPE -- without
a hook per command, and without the core growing one every time a command
is added.

  module_add_command_hook(mod, HOOK_COMMAND_PRE, "KICK", on_kick,
                          HOOK_PRIORITY_DEFAULT, 0, 0);

They are registered with module_add_command_hook() and not with
module_add_hook(), which refuses them: a command hook carries a command
name, and a hook registered without one is handed every line the server
parses -- all the server-to-server traffic included -- to throw almost all
of it away.  Pass NULL as the command only if that is really what you want.

The context carries a struct HookCommand in hc_command:

  static enum HookResult on_kick(struct HookContext *ctx, void *user)
  {
    const struct HookCommand *cmd = ctx->hc_command;

    cmd->hcc_cmd      /* "KICK" */
    cmd->hcc_tok      /* "K", its P10 token */
    cmd->hcc_handler  /* CLIENT_HANDLER, SERVER_HANDLER, OPER_HANDLER, ... */
    cmd->hcc_parc     /* number of parameters */
    cmd->hcc_parv     /* the parameters; parv[0] is the source */
    cmd->hcc_result   /* POST only: what the handler returned */
    ...
  }

and the usual context fields are filled in with the subject the server
resolved:

  ctx->hc_source    /* who ran the command */
  ctx->hc_client    /* who it acts on, already a struct Client* */
  ctx->hc_channel   /* the channel it names, already a struct Channel* */
  ctx->hc_arg       /* its reason, or the mask a *-line names */

That resolution is the point.  A module that wanted this before would have
had to parse parv itself, and would have had to know that the same KICK
carries a nick from a client and a numnick from a server -- a distinction
every ms_*() handler in the tree makes and every module would have got
wrong for one command in ten.  Which parameter means what is declared by
the command, in msgtab[] (see struct MsgSubject in msg.h), so a command
registered by a module can declare it too.

A command that declares nothing still reaches the hooks; hc_client and
hc_channel are simply NULL and the module reads hcc_parv.

Five rules, and the reason for each:

  * A command whose source is a service bot (+S) reaches neither point.  A
    service acts on the server's behalf, and auditing or vetoing it is
    auditing the server.  A module that does want to see them -- an audit
    log -- passes HOOK_CMD_INCLUDE_SERVICES as the flags.

  * HOOK_DENY at HOOK_COMMAND_PRE is honoured only when the source is a
    client of this server.  For a command that arrived from another server
    the point is a notification: the rest of the network has already
    applied it, and refusing it here would desynchronise this server rather
    than prevent anything.  The attempt is logged.

  * HOOK_COMMAND_POST does not run when the handler returned CPTR_KILLED.
    The client is gone by then and the pointers in the context would be
    freed memory; a module learns about the exit from HOOK_CLIENT_EXITING,
    which is where it belongs.  It does not run for a command a hook
    refused either: nothing happened to report.

  * hcc_parv is read-only.  Rewriting an arbitrary parameter of a command
    in flight is not something the server can bound the consequences of.
    What a module may change is the context: a numeric and a reason when it
    denies, and nothing else.

  * A hook that causes another command to be dispatched is bounded.  Past a
    small depth the server stops running the hooks and logs it, rather than
    running out of stack on a module that wrote a loop without noticing.

Denying works the same as at every other veto point: set hc_numeric and
hc_reason, return HOOK_DENY, and the client is told in the module's own
words.

  ctx->hc_numeric = ERR_CHANOPRIVSNEEDED;
  ircd_strncpy(ctx->hc_reason, "that user is an operator",
               sizeof(ctx->hc_reason) - 1);
  return HOOK_DENY;

modules/hooks/cmdaudit.c is the reference: it logs what one user does to
another and refuses a KICK aimed at a network service or an operator.


VETO HOOKS ONLY SEE LOCAL CLIENTS
=================================

This is the most important thing to understand about hooks, so it gets its
own section.

Every veto hook runs only on the path taken by clients connected to THIS
server.  A nick change, join or message arriving from another server has
already been accepted by the rest of the network; refusing it here would
leave this server disagreeing with everyone else about what a user is
called or which channel they are in, which is a desynchronisation that
does not heal by itself.

So:

  * A veto binds the server that ran it, not the network.
  * A user on another server can do what your module forbids, and your
    server will see it happen.
  * If you want a rule to apply network-wide, every server needs the
    module, or the rule belongs somewhere else -- a G-line, a channel
    mode, or a service.

Notification hooks do not have this restriction; some fire for remote
clients too, because a notification cannot desynchronise anything.


REFUSING, AND SAYING WHY
========================

  ctx->hc_numeric = ERR_CANNOTSENDTOCHAN;    /* optional */
  ircd_strncpy(ctx->hc_reason, "Because I said so",
               sizeof(ctx->hc_reason) - 1);   /* optional */
  return HOOK_DENY;

Both fields are optional.  Leaving hc_numeric at zero lets the server pick
one that suits the hook point, and leaving hc_reason empty produces a
generic message.


REWRITING A MESSAGE
===================

Only MESSAGE_PRE_CHANNEL and MESSAGE_PRE_PRIVATE can rewrite.

  if (ctx->hc_rewrite && ctx->hc_rewrite_len > 0) {
    /* write into the server's buffer, never assign your own pointer */
    ircd_strncpy(ctx->hc_rewrite, new_text, ctx->hc_rewrite_len - 1);
    ctx->hc_rewrite[ctx->hc_rewrite_len - 1] = '\0';
    ctx->hc_rewritten = 1;
  }

The server owns the buffer and tells you how big it is.  Assigning your
own pointer to hc_rewrite would leave the server reading memory your
module may have freed, or that goes away entirely when the module unloads.

Rewriting is not a veto: return HOOK_CONTINUE so the rest of the chain
still runs.


WHAT A MODULE MUST NOT DO
=========================

  * Block.  The server is single-threaded and hooks run in the middle of
    handling a client's command.  A hook that waits on a network round
    trip or a slow disk stalls every other client.  Hand the slow part to
    a worker thread and act on the answer when it arrives -- see WORKER
    THREADS below and doc/readme.workers -- and, where the hook point
    offers a token, suspend the operation while that happens rather than
    deciding without the answer; see SUSPENDING A HOOK.

  * Unload itself.  Unloading from inside a module callback would free the
    code that is executing.  The server refuses and logs it.

  * Keep a pointer into the core across an unload, or hand the core a
    pointer into itself that outlives the module.

  * Assume another module is loaded, or assume a load order beyond hook
    priorities.


WORKER THREADS
==============

A module that needs to wait for something -- a database, an HTTP API, a
password hash -- cannot wait in a command handler or a hook, because both
run in the main thread in the middle of serving a client.  It submits the
work instead and is called back when the answer is ready:

  #include "worker.h"

  struct WorkTask *task = worker_task_new(my_work, my_done);
  task->wt_in = payload;                /* from worker_alloc() */
  worker_task_set_client(task, sptr);   /* the numnick, not the pointer */

  if (!module_submit_work(mod, task))
    worker_task_free(task);             /* refused: still ours */

my_work runs in a worker thread; my_done runs in the main thread, where
touching core state is safe again.  For something that is not a series of
short tasks -- a listening socket, a subscription -- module_spawn_worker()
starts a thread with a loop of its own.

Everything a module registers is reverted when it unloads, and work is no
exception: queued tasks are dropped, running ones are waited for, finished
results are discarded rather than delivered, and dedicated workers are
stopped.  Waiting is not optional -- the module's code is about to be
unmapped -- so unloading a module with slow work in flight blocks the whole
server until that work returns.  It is logged when it happens.

Both calls need FEAT_WORKER_THREADS to be non-zero, which it is not by
default, and both fail gracefully when it is zero.  Check the return value
and tell the operator, rather than assuming.

There is one rule for code that runs in a worker thread, and it admits no
exceptions: it never touches core state.  No struct Client, no struct
Channel, no CurrentTime, no MyMalloc(), no log_write(), no send_reply().
Read doc/readme.workers before writing any of it -- the list of what is
allowed is short, and breaking the rule produces corruption that surfaces
somewhere else entirely.


A DATABASE
==========

A module that wants a database does not need a worker thread of its own.
Load a database driver -- modules/workers/postgres is the one that ships --
and call the API in include/db.h:

  #include "db.h"

  struct DbParam  nick  = { DB_TYPE_TEXT, cli_name(sptr), DB_FORMAT_TEXT };
  struct DbParam* args[] = { &nick, NULL };
  struct DbQuery  query = { "select score from accounts where nick = $1",
                            args };

  if (db_query(mod, &query, account_loaded, numnick) != DB_OK)
    /* refused here and now; account_loaded will not run */;

account_loaded runs in the main thread, up to five seconds later, with the
rows as a json_t array.  The statement is prepared and the values are
bound, so a parameter can never be read as SQL; the driver's own types
never appear; and unloading the module drops its pending callbacks instead
of calling into code that is no longer mapped, so a module with queries in
flight is still safe to unload.

The driver is configured by a Database{} block in ircd.conf, which the
server keeps whether or not any driver is loaded.  See doc/readme.database.


SQL MIGRATIONS
==============

A module that needs tables of its own ships the SQL that makes them, in a
migrations/ subdirectory of its own directory:

  modules/<type>/<name>/migrations/v1_create_accounts.up.sql
  modules/<type>/<name>/migrations/v1_create_accounts.down.sql

The build compiles them into the shared object -- they are never copied
beside it -- and the server checks them when the module is loaded: the
v<N>_<name>.<up|down>.sql spelling, a name of letters, digits and
underscores, versions running from 1 with no gaps, and an up for every
down.  A module that breaks any of that does not load, and the operator is
told which file is wrong.

Nothing runs by itself.  An operator applies and reverts with /MODULE
MIGRATION; the server never does it on load, on reload or on start-up.  The
one exception is the server's own set, which creates the table the rest are
recorded in, and which is why no module may be called "core".

A module reads its own tables with the database API in include/db.h; see
A DATABASE above.  Read doc/readme.migrations before shipping any of it --
the rules are short and the loader enforces all of them.


TRANSLATIONS
============

A module that talks to users can ship translations of what it says: one
GNU PO file per language in a po/ subdirectory of its own directory,

  modules/<type>/<name>/po/es.po
  modules/<type>/<name>/po/pt.po

They are ordinary resources -- copied beside the shared object, never
compiled in -- and the loader opens them as the module's own domain before
mi_init, so module_i18n(mod) is good from there.  Nothing crosses between
domains: a module's text is looked up in the module's catalogs and nowhere
else.  A single-file module has no directory and therefore no translations,
the same rule that applies to its migrations.

  #define I18N_DOMAIN mod_i18n           /* before the include */
  #include "ircd_i18n.h"

  static struct I18nDomain* mod_i18n;    /* = module_i18n(mod) in mi_init */

  sendcmdto_one(&me, CMD_NOTICE, sptr, _(sptr, "%C :Bot %s destroyed"),
                sptr, name);

_() and _n() then translate with the module's domain for the client given;
a NULL domain -- no po/ installed -- returns the original, so a module never
tests for it.  A broken catalog is reported and does not stop the load; a
/REHASH reloads the module's catalogs with the core's, and /MODULE RELOAD
reopens the domain.  The loader checks every translation's ircd_snprintf
directives against the original before it is used, so a translator cannot
crash a module.  doc/readme.translations has the rules, the marking
conventions and the `pot` target that writes the module's template.


A LIBRARY OF ITS OWN
====================

A module links against nothing by default: its symbols resolve against the
ircd.  One that needs more than that -- a client library, a header outside
the tree -- says so in a CMake fragment beside its sources rather than in
the core's build files:

  modules/<type>/<name>/module.cmake      a module built from a directory
  modules/<type>/<name>.cmake             a single-file module

The fragment is read with IRCU_MODULE_NAME, IRCU_MODULE_TYPE and
IRCU_MODULE_DIR set, and answers by setting IRCU_MODULE_LINK_LIBRARIES,
IRCU_MODULE_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES, IRCU_MODULE_COMPILE_DEFINITIONS or
IRCU_MODULE_COMPILE_OPTIONS -- or IRCU_MODULE_SKIP, with a reason, to bow
out when an optional dependency is not installed.  A skipped module is
reported at configure time and stops nothing else from building.

modules/workers/postgres/module.cmake is the worked example: it looks for
libpq and jansson, and skips the module when either is missing.


THE EXAMPLES
============

  commands/example_cmd/  adds a command; the minimal reference, and the
                         reference for a module built from a directory:
                         two sources, a private header and a resource
                         (hello.txt) it reads through module_dir()
  hooks/trace_events.c   logs every notification hook; noisy, for
                         debugging a hook point
  hooks/nickpolicy.c     refuses nicknames; a worked veto
  hooks/slowauth.c       holds a registration while a worker thread
                         answers for it; the reference for HOOK_PENDING
  hooks/nocaps.c         refuses shouted channel messages and lowercases
                         shouted private ones; a veto and a rewrite
  modes/umode_nopm.c     adds user mode +P and gives it a meaning; the
                         reference for registering a user mode
  modes/cmode_nocaps.c   adds channel mode +G and gives it a meaning; the
                         reference for registering a channel mode, and
                         nocaps.c's rule made asked-for instead of
                         always-on
  workers/worker_demo.c  hashes a password in a worker thread; the
                         reference for module_submit_work() and for
                         referring to a client that may have left
  workers/worker_ticker.c
                         runs a thread with a loop of its own; the
                         reference for module_spawn_worker()
  workers/postgres/      the database driver behind include/db.h; the
                         reference for a module with a connection pool,
                         for a module with libraries of its own
                         (module.cmake), and for one whose worker threads
                         outlive a single task
  commands/m_bot/        adds /BOT, virtual bots operators create on
                         demand; the reference for using the bot API
                         (see VIRTUAL CLIENTS below) and for a module
                         that ships translations (po/es.po) and marks
                         its notices with _()
  services/irc_services/ network services -- NickServ, ChanServ --
                         embedded in the server: service bots from
                         Service{} blocks, answering users through
                         HOOK_MESSAGE_RECEIVED; the reference for
                         service bots, for HOOK_CONFIG_LOADED, and for
                         a module that brings a client back when the
                         network takes it away (doc/readme.services)

All paths are under modules/.


VIRTUAL CLIENTS
===============

A module can bring a user into existence that has no connection behind
it -- a bot, a service -- through include/bot.h:

  if (bot_check_nick(nick, NULL) == 0)
    bot = bot_create(mod, nick, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0);
  bot_join(bot, "#channel");                 /* with ops, creating it */
  bot_send_user(bot, acptr, 1, "hello");     /* 1: NOTICE, 0: PRIVMSG */
  bot_send_channel(bot, chptr, 0, "hello");
  bot_set_user_mode(bot, acptr, "+r");       /* service bots only, see below */
  bot_destroy(bot, &me, "reason");

The bot is a struct Client made with make_client(&me, ...), so it shares
the server's own Connection, which has no descriptor: cli_from() of it
is &me and everything sent to it is dropped in can_send() before
anything is queued.  Its server is &me, so it takes a numnick from this
server's own range and the network sees a user of this server.  It is
not MyConnect(), so the socket paths in exit_client() are never taken.
From then on set_nick_name(), the JoinBuf and the ModeBuf treat it like
any other client, and neither bot.c nor the module speaks P10 itself.

Two things follow from sharing &me's Connection, and bot.c respects
both.  Never write a per-connection field of such a client --
cli_handler(), cli_snomask(), cli_privs() and the like all belong to
&me -- and send what it says through send.c (bot_send_*) rather than
the relay layer, whose flood limits assert on a client that is not
local.

The first argument to bot_create() is the owner.  Unloading a module
destroys the bots it owns, so no bot outlives the code that answers for
it; a module that wants a quit reason of its own destroys them from
mi_fini first, as m_bot does.  The server may also take a bot away
without asking -- a /KILL from another server, a nick collision it
loses -- and a module that keeps a pointer attaches to
HOOK_CLIENT_EXITING, where bot_find() still answers for the client on
its way out, and drops the pointer there.

Every bot carries user mode +B, a bot of the network.  Pass BOT_SERVICE
in the flags for a bot that is a service of the network -- a NickServ --
and it is introduced with +S, +k and +o as well: +k for everything the
server already grants a channel service (no KICK, no deop, no local
KILL, no target limits), +o because a service acts with an operator's
standing, +S so that messages sent to it reach its module through
HOOK_MESSAGE_RECEIVED and so that /BOT will neither rename nor destroy
it.  +B and +S are the server's alone: a user cannot set or clear them,
and neither can an operator; only a MODE from another server is
believed.  A bot with +S is what doc/readme.services is about.

A service bot may also change the user modes of anyone who is not an
operator, through bot_set_user_mode(); what it is for is +r, the mark
of a user identified to its nick, which nobody but a server or a
service bot can grant (doc/readme.accounting).  It is held to the modes
a user may set on itself, plus +r and -r: +o, +k, +S, +B and -x are
dropped from whatever it asks for.  The change reaches the network and
the user as coming from the bot.  Any bot, +S or not, may change its
own modes the same way, within the same limits.


DIAGNOSTICS
===========

  /MODULE LIST     module name, declared name, version, ABI, path,
                   description and who loaded it (the operator's nick, or
                   the configuration file)
  /MODULE MIGRATION LIST
                   every row of the migrations table: what has actually
                   been applied to this database, by whom, when, and how
                   long it took
  /MODULE MIGRATION STATUS <module>
                   what that module ships, and which of it is applied
  /STATS M         loaded modules, how many commands, user modes and
                   channel modes each registered (with the mode letters
                   for both), who loaded each one,
                   for each hook point in use how many hooks are
                   attached and how many times it has fired,
                   the state of the worker pool, and for any module using
                   workers how much work it has in flight

The hook call counts are the quickest way to tell a hook that is not
firing from one that is firing and abstaining.
